po-bot","email":"brent+expobotnpm@expo.io"},{"name":"philpl","email":"phil@kitten.sh"},{"name":"ccheever","email":"ccheever@gmail.com"},{"name":"szdziedzic","email":"sz.dziedz@gmail.com"},{"name":"wschurman","email":"wschurman@gmail.com"}],"readme":"<p>\n  <a href=\"https://docs.expo.dev/modules/\">\n    <img\n      src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/expo/expo-modules-macros/main/.github/resources/expo-modules-macros.svg\"\n      alt=\"expo-modules-macros\"\n      height=\"64\" />\n  </a>\n</p>\n\n# @expo/modules-macros-linux-arm64\n\nThe Linux arm64 build of the `expo-modules-macros` compiler plugin and scanner (`ExpoModulesMacros`).\n\nDon't install this package directly. `expo-modules-macros` lists it as an optional dependency, and package managers install it only on Linux arm64 with glibc, because of its `os`, `cpu` and `libc` fields. `getScannerBinaryPath()` in `expo-modules-macros` returns the path of the executable in it.\n\nThe Swift runtime is linked in statically, so the executable runs without a Swift toolchain. It needs glibc 2.34 or newer and the GNU C++ runtime (`libstdc++` and `libgcc_s`), for example on Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12, Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9 or Amazon Linux 2023, or a newer release of one of them. It doesn't run on musl distributions such as Alpine Linux.\n","readmeFilename":"README.md"}